        "deliverables": [
          {
            "id": "deliverable:obj_1790046504201_vporlj:launch/page-message",
            "title": "Launch page headline and message",
            "type": "document",
            "version": 3,
            "status": "current",
            "summary": "Version 3: retained the existing review context and diagnosed problems, clarified the audience and offer, supplied a coherent revised messaging package, and added explicit comparative success criteria and a decision rule. Draft remains for…",
            "content": "Launch page — messaging review and revised draft\nVersion: 3 (draft for review; not published)\n\nEvidence base\n- Internal launch context (company record: launch/context; observation ev_1790046568153_806r86 and current observation ev_1790046600094_5fa1zo): the goal is to convert one-person-company founders; the current signal is low signup conversion and visitors do not recognise themselves.\n- Existing draft evidence base: founder narrative pulse (verified acquired input previously cited in version 2 as sim_result_6269e01f64377bbfb5f17971): founders describe switching between selling, researching, following up, and doing the work; “AI manager” can sound like another dashboard or advisor; generic “automate your workflows” feels broad and tool-like; concrete outcome/accountability language is easier to understand.\n\nPrincipal messaging problems, prioritized by likely impact\n1. Highest impact — audience mismatch and weak recognition. The current headline says “for your business” and the page assumes generic “businesses,” while the launch goal is one-person-company founders. This aligns with the observed low signup conversion and visitors not recognising themselves.\n2. High impact — the pain is framed as tools/workflows rather than the founder’s daily operating load. “AI manager” and generic automation language can sound abstract, dashboard-like, or tool-led.\n3. High impact — the promised outcome and reason to act are not concrete enough. The message should make the founder’s practical gain—keeping selling, research, follow-up, and delivery moving—immediately legible.\n\nRevised messaging package\n\nAudience\nOne-person-company founders and lean SME operators who personally move between selling, researching, following up, and delivering the work. The page should speak directly to the founder, not to a generic business or an enterprise department.\n\nOffer framing\nSomebody is an AI manager for one-person companies and lean SMEs. It helps the founder keep important work moving across the operating load instead of presenting itself primarily as another dashboard, generic workflow tool, or abstract automation promise.\n\nHeadline\nRun your one-person company without dropping the balls.\n\nSubhead\nSomebody helps you keep selling, researching, following up, and doing the work moving—so you can stay accountable to what matters without managing another maze of tools.\n\nPrimary call to action\nSee how Somebody can run alongside your work\n\nSupporting copy\nYou are the salesperson, researcher, follow-up person, and delivery team. Somebody helps turn the work you need to move into clear next actions and keeps the thread visible as you get on with the business.\n\nWhy this message\n- Names the intended audience directly: one-person-company founders.\n- Reflects the diagnosed recognition problem rather than speaking to a generic “business.”\n- Leads with the founder’s concrete operating load instead of generic workflow automation.\n- Explains the offer in outcome and accountability language while retaining the AI-manager positioning.\n- Avoids claiming that Somebody replaces the founder or guarantees a business result.\n\nExplicit criteria for determining whether it is better\nRun the revised message against the current message with the same audience, traffic conditions, placement, and test window where practical. The revised message is better only if:\n1. Signup conversion rate is higher than the current message, with the comparison method, sample size, and test window recorded before the test.\n2. Audience recognition improves: in an on-page or post-visit recognition measure, more visitors identify themselves as one-person-company founders or say the page is clearly for them than under the current message.\n3. Offer comprehension is not degraded: respondents can accurately describe that Somebody is an AI manager for one-person companies/lean SMEs that helps keep their work moving.\n4. The qualitative feedback no longer primarily describes the offer as generic automation, another dashboard, or a tool/workflow product; feedback should instead show understanding of the concrete operating outcome.\n5. No material deterioration is observed in relevant downstream signup quality or activation checks available for the test.\n\nDecision rule\nPrefer the revised message when it improves signup conversion and audience recognition versus the current message, while meeting the offer-comprehension and downstream-quality guardrails above. If conversion rises but recognition, comprehension, or downstream quality worsens, do not declare the message better; revise and retest. Record the final comparison data and qualitative findings before approval.\n\nStatus\nDraft for review only. Not sent or published.",
            "updatedAt": 1790046616989
          }
        ],
